<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-functions-complexity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Functions and Complexity</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
    You are walking through the campus and you bump into one of your friends who
    is excited to tell you that they have just learned in their discrete math
    class that
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          the set <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m> has exactly <m>32</m> subsets,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          there are as many natural numbers as there are even natural numbers,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          there are more numbers in the interval <m>(0,1)</m> than in the set of
          natural numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    You wonder how can it be
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          that there are a lot more subsets than elements in a small set like
          <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          that the set of even natural numbers <m>2\N</m> is a proper subset of
          the set <m>\N</m> of natural numbers and these two sets have the same
          size, and
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          that you have more numbers in <m>(0,1)</m> than in <m>\N</m>, aren't
          all the infinities the same?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    Your friend also tells you that in their discrete math class they also
    learned that
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          any set with <m>n</m> elements has exactly <m>2^{n}</m> subsets,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          there are as many natural numbers as there are integers,
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          there are different kinds of infinities.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    This makes you curious and you decide to learn more about this topic. You
    start with some notions that will help the understanding of the
    relationships between sets.
  </p>
  </introduction>

